          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "A three-character ICD-10-CM category is only a usable (billable) code if it has no more specific subdivisions below it. The retrieved material supports this only through examples, not a general rule.\n\n**What the sources say**\n\n- A code is three to seven characters long. The three-character form is the category, and characters four and five add detail such as cause, site, or severity. Character six often records laterality (right, left, or unspecified). (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- Most categories need a fourth, fifth, or sixth character before they are billable. I10 (essential hypertension) is an exception: it is complete at three characters, so no further detail can be added. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n- Asthma (J45) is never used at three characters. Its remaining characters come from a severity axis and an encounter-state axis. (Source: *Coding asthma: J45 and its severity and exacerbation axes*.)\n- Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (J44) is also split further by its fourth character, which records whether a complication is present. (Source: *Coding chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: J44 and its two complication codes*.)\n- Type 2 diabetes (E11) has complication subcategories below the category level. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n\n**What the sources did not cover**\n\nThe passages did not give a complete test for every category. They did not explain how to check a specific category against the official code tables, which is the authoritative way to confirm whether it is billable. For that, the official ICD-10-CM tabular list published by the US National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS) and CMS is the place to check. Coding decisions for real records should be made or checked by a trained medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
